# Summary
Introduced bug: the shard repair worker uses a polling wait that may return None on timeout, but the worker interprets that as a terminal condition rather than continuing to wait or performing another durable scan.
The commit adds a background shard repair sweeper. Its main loop attempts to take queued repair work and otherwise calls wait_for_placed_segment_shard_repair_work. The local wait helper uses a bounded condvar wait and returns None when the queue is still empty after the timeout. The sweeper treats None as a shutdown signal and breaks out of the loop. As a result, a normally idle server can lose its shard repair worker shortly after startup. Any repair rows created later may remain durable but unprocessed until the process restarts or another worker is spawned. This weakens the intended durability repair behavior and can leave recoverable shard corruption unrepaired, but it does not appear to create a remotely exploitable confidentiality, authentication, or authorization issue under the provided threat model.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the worker loop treats `None` from repair-work waiting as terminal shutdown/break rather than retrying.
- [x] Confirm the public wait path delegates to a single local polling wait rather than looping until stop/work.
- [x] Confirm the local polling wait returns `None` after a bounded timeout with an empty queue and `stop=false`.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce that a worker using this loop exits during idle time and fails to process repair work enqueued afterward.
- [x] Capture concrete evidence through a crashing/failing PoC test and debugger trace; note valgrind was unavailable.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC. The vulnerable control flow is present: `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:526-533` breaks the shard repair worker loop when the combined `try_take(...).or_else(wait...)` returns `None`; `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:6835-6842` delegates directly to one local wait; and `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:1172-1194` performs only a single bounded `Condvar::wait_timeout` and then returns `None` via `pop_front()?` if the queue is still empty while `stop=false`. I added `poc_shard_repair_worker_exits_after_idle_poll_timeout` to `crates/storage/src/cluster/local/tests.rs`, which mimics the worker loop, starts with an empty queue and `stop=false`, sleeps past the 100ms poll timeout, then enqueues repair work. The test failed as expected: `processed=0` and `remaining=Some(PlacedSegmentShardRepairWorkItem ...)`, proving the worker exited during normal idleness and left later repair work queued. I also attempted instrumentation/debug validation: `valgrind` was unavailable in the container, and a non-interactive LLDB run stopped at the failing assertion with a backtrace into the PoC test.

